+{"id":"24e41cd3-a0aa-4803-a13a-e301486dbc10","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:20:35.021000","source":"agent","parent_id":"1235087e-41da-4fc3-8cdc-fd7c42f8012a","thought":[{"cache_prompt":false,"type":"text","text":"Now let me look at the argparse/main section and the exact ots-handling lines:"}],"reasoning_content":"Now I need to design the feature.\n\nThe requirement: add opt-in `--max-pending-hours H` to `check-anchors`.\n- Without option, pending proofs accepted (exit 0).\n- With option, if any range head's proof is still pending and its Sigstore attestation time is more than H hours ago, exit 1. Print to stderr a message containing `still pending N h`, N whole number of hours since the attestation.\n- Pending proof younger than H hours is fine (exit 0).\n\nWhere does the Sigstore attestation time come from? The OTS proof file... Hmm, \"its Sigstore attestation time\" — the Sigstore attestation for that range head (anchors/NNNNNN.sigstore.json). The check_ots returns earliest Bitcoin block time or None if pending. When pending, we should look at the Sigstore attestation's integratedTime for the range head, and check how long ago it is.\n\nNote the test `test_a_missing_ots_proof_for_a_range_head_is_caught` stubs check_ots and check_sigstore. With check_sigstore stubbed to return (1, range_predicate(...), \"c0ffee\") — t=1, that's ancient. Hmm, that test doesn't use --max-pending-hours so it should be unaffected (no option → pending accepted, exit 0). Good.\n\nDesign: in cmd_check_anchors, track pending anchors (ots proofs still pending, t None). When `a.max_pending_hours` is set, for each pending anchor, get the Sigstore attestation time for that seq (via check_sigstore on the .sigstore.json file) and compute hours since.\n\nBut check_sigstore may raise Bad/CantCheck. We should handle gracefully: if we can't get attestation time, probably skip or treat... Let's keep simple: for pending proofs, try to fetch the sigstore attestation; use integratedTime.\n\nActually maybe